# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# /squad-analysis is the name the product docs use for the deep squad view, and
# without this it hit the catch-all and quietly served the dashboard instead —
# a 200 with the wrong page, which is worse than a 404 because nothing looks
# broken. 200 rewrites rather than 301s, so the clean URL is served in place and
# the existing filename keeps working for every link already pointing at it.
#
# Hash fragments never reach the server, so /squad-analysis#transfers?out=..&in=..
# carries the dashboard's pre-loaded transfer through unchanged.

/squad-analysis      /fpl-my-team-analysis.html   200
/squad-analysis/*    /fpl-my-team-analysis.html   200

# The standalone Transfer Wizard is retired. It shipped a diverged, older copy of
# the transfer engine — calculateTransferScore was 40 lines there against 242 in
# scripts/transfer-wizard.js — so two features with the same name gave different
# answers. It was also the last page on the legacy nav.html, which meant every
# nav change had to be made twice. 301 rather than a rewrite: the page is gone,
# and search engines should be told so.
/fpl-transfer-wizard.html   /fpl-my-team-analysis.html   301
/fpl-transfer-wizard        /fpl-my-team-analysis.html   301

# The dashboard is index.html in its signed-in state, and index.html rewrites
# its own URL to /dashboard so a browser tab and a bookmark say which of the
# two pages you are looking at. That URL has to survive a refresh, so it is
# served in place rather than redirected — a 301 to / would undo the rename on
# every reload. Same 200-rewrite shape as /squad-analysis above, and for the
# same reason: the clean URL is the one people end up sharing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- /dashboard
#
# Every signed-in screen lives under /dashboard/. It used to be only the home
# screen, with the other six served from their own .html at the site root — so
# the product's URLs were half one thing and half another, and no path said
# "this is the app" except the one.
#
# 200 rewrites, not 301s: the clean URL is served in place, so a refresh keeps
# it. Each page's assets are root-absolute for this reason — a relative
# styles/common.css under /dashboard/players resolves to
# /dashboard/styles/common.css and 404s.
#
# Order matters here: the named pages must precede the /dashboard/* catch-all,
# or every one of them would be served the home screen instead.
/dashboard/login          /welcome.html                 200
/dashboard/my-team        /fpl-my-team-analysis.html    200
/dashboard/squad          /fpl-my-team-analysis.html    200
/dashboard/players        /fpl-players-analysis.html    200
/dashboard/teams          /fpl-teams-analysis.html      200
/dashboard/rivals         /fpl-league-rivals.html       200
/dashboard/scouts-desk    /fpl-scouts-desk.html         200
/dashboard/news           /fpl-news.html                200
/dashboard/premium        /premium.html                 200

# The old root paths keep working so nothing already linked or bookmarked
# breaks; 301 because the /dashboard/ URL is the canonical one now.
/fpl-my-team-analysis.html   /dashboard/my-team       301
/fpl-players-analysis.html   /dashboard/players       301
/fpl-teams-analysis.html     /dashboard/teams         301
/fpl-league-rivals.html      /dashboard/rivals        301
/fpl-scouts-desk.html        /dashboard/scouts-desk   301
/fpl-news.html               /dashboard/news          301
/welcome.html                /dashboard/login         301

# Someone opening /dashboard without a saved Team ID is sent to the ID page by
# the gate in common.js; the rewrite here only decides which file answers.
/dashboard      /index.html   200
/dashboard/     /index.html   200

# And nothing else under it exists. There used to be a /dashboard/* catch-all
# here, which answered every unknown path with index.html at 200 and so made
# /dashboard/ a generator of pages that are not missing: /dashboard/anything
# returned the home screen, and the relative fetches on it resolved to
# /dashboard/data/*.json and got 200 and a page of HTML where the code
# expected JSON — the bug that showed the landing page with a sidebar over it.
# A crawler that finds one of these sees a soft 404: a real page, with real
# content, at a URL that means nothing.
#
# Deleted rather than replaced with a rule pointing at 404.html. With no rule
# matching, an unknown path under /dashboard/ finds no file and Pages serves
# the site's own 404.html with a 404 — the behaviour we want, out of the
# default path, with nothing here that has to be right for it to happen.

# NOT the extensionless spellings. /fpl-my-team-analysis and /welcome were
# 301'd here too, for a day, so that the canonical on each of those pages named
# a URL that redirected rather than one that quietly served a duplicate. It
# broke signing in, and the mechanism is worth writing down.
#
# Pages serves /dashboard/login by rewriting it to /welcome.html, and it serves
# an asset at its clean URL — /welcome.html IS /welcome. Adding
#
#     #
# meant the rewrite's own target was the source of a redirect back to where the
# request came from: /dashboard/login -> /welcome -> /dashboard/login, forever.
# The .html spellings above have the same shape on paper and do not do this,
# which is what made it look safe; the difference is that the rewrite target is
# already the .html form, so nothing normalises it into a rule.
#
# The duplicate those rules were removing is a page that is noindex and not in
# the sitemap, which is a paper cut. A login loop is not. tests/redirect-cycles
# fails the build if one comes back.
